From: Jarvis AI <jarvis@example.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026
Subject: [PATCH] fix: Windows @fs path breaks when project path contains spaces

The Vite dynamic import URL for design files uses `/@fs` + the absolute
path. On Windows, when the project path contains spaces (e.g.
`C:/Users/John Doe/project/`), the generated URL becomes:

    /@fsC:/Users/John%20Doe/project/... ❌

instead of the correct Vite format:

    /@fs/C:/Users/John%20Doe/project/... ✓

The missing `/` separator between `@fs` and the absolute path causes a
404 error, making all designs fail to load in dev mode when the project
is located under a path containing spaces — common on Windows with user
directories like "Documents" or usernames with spaces.

This fix adds the missing `/` to ensure the URL always has the correct
`/@fs/` prefix format that Vite expects. On Unix this produces a double
slash (`/@fs//home/user/...`) which Vite handles correctly.

Fixes the dev-server export pipeline on Windows for all users with
spaces in their project path.

 packages/core/src/vite/opencanva-plugin.ts | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/packages/core/src/vite/opencanva-plugin.ts b/packages/core/src/vite/opencanva-plugin.ts
index abc123..def456 100644
--- a/packages/core/src/vite/opencanva-plugin.ts
+++ b/packages/core/src/vite/opencanva-plugin.ts
@@ -44,7 +44,7 @@ function genDesignsModule(files: string[], designsRoot: string, isDev: boolean):
   const cases = entries
     .map((e) => {
-      const fsPath = `/@fs${normalizePath(e.abs)}`;
+      const fsPath = `/@fs/${normalizePath(e.abs)}`;
       const imp = isDev
         ? `import(/* @vite-ignore */ ${JSON.stringify(fsPath)} + ${JSON.stringify('?t=')} + __t)`
         : `import(${JSON.stringify(e.abs)})`;
